__d(function(g,r,_i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"listeningLessons",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return h}});var t=r(d[0]),n=r(d[1]);const i=(t,n,i,o,s=1)=>({id:`q${t}`,type:"gap",prompt:n,answers:i,explanation:o,maxWords:s}),o=(t,n,i,o,s,h="choice")=>({id:`q${t}`,type:h,prompt:n,options:i,answers:[o],explanation:s}),s=t=>({kind:"bundled",src:`/task1-coach-pages/listening/${t}.mp3`}),h=[...[{id:"riverside-booking",title:"A weekend on the river",subtitle:"\u0110\u1eb7t ch\u1ed7 cho m\u1ed9t chuy\u1ebfn ch\xe8o thuy\u1ec1n",part:1,format:"Form completion",instructions:"Complete the booking form. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.",media:s("riverside-booking"),transcript:"Receptionist: Good morning, Riverside Outdoor Centre. How can I help?\nVisitor: Hello. I would like to book a beginner kayaking session for two people. We have never tried it before.\nReceptionist: Certainly. Let me take your surname first.\nVisitor: It is Carter. That is C, A, R, T, E, R.\nReceptionist: Thank you. Our next beginner session is on Saturday. There is also an advanced group on Sunday, but Saturday is the right one for you.\nVisitor: Saturday is fine. When should we arrive?\nReceptionist: The session starts at nine thirty, but please arrive fifteen minutes earlier to complete the paperwork. The total cost is forty eight pounds for two people, including all the equipment.\nVisitor: Forty eight pounds. Good. Where do we meet the instructor?\nReceptionist: At the bridge, not the main reception building. Follow the signs from the car park and you will see our blue flag there. Your instructor is called Helen. She will check your equipment before you get on the water.\nVisitor: Should we bring anything particular?\nReceptionist: A towel is useful because you may get wet. We provide waterproof jackets, so you do not need to bring those. Please wear trainers rather than sandals.\nVisitor: Is there somewhere to get lunch afterwards?\nReceptionist: Yes. You can buy lunch at the cafe beside the entrance. It serves soup and sandwiches. The boats return at twelve fifteen, so you will have plenty of time to eat.\nVisitor: Perfect. Can I pay when we arrive?\nReceptionist: Yes, but we accept payment by card only. The card machine is at the meeting point. Please do not bring cash for the booking.\nVisitor: All right. I look forward to it.",questions:[i(1,"Surname: ______",["Carter"],"Carter \u0111\u01b0\u1ee3c \u0111\xe1nh v\u1ea7n C-A-R-T-E-R."),i(2,"Day of the beginner session: ______",["Saturday"],"Saturday d\xe0nh cho ng\u01b0\u1eddi m\u1edbi; Sunday l\xe0 nh\xf3m n\xe2ng cao."),i(3,"Session begins at: ______",["9:30","9.30","09:30","nine thirty"],"9:30 l\xe0 gi\u1edd b\u1eaft \u0111\u1ea7u; \u0111\u1ebfn s\u1edbm 15 ph\xfat kh\xf4ng ph\u1ea3i \u0111\xe1p \xe1n.",2),i(4,"Total price for two people: \xa3______",["48","forty-eight","forty eight"],"T\u1ed5ng gi\xe1 cho hai ng\u01b0\u1eddi l\xe0 48 b\u1ea3ng.",2),i(5,"Meeting point: the ______",["bridge"],"G\u1eb7p t\u1ea1i bridge, kh\xf4ng ph\u1ea3i reception."),i(6,"Instructor\u2019s first name: ______",["Helen"],"T\xean ng\u01b0\u1eddi h\u01b0\u1edbng d\u1eabn l\xe0 Helen."),i(7,"Bring a ______ because you may get wet.",["towel"],"Trung t\xe2m cung c\u1ea5p \xe1o ch\u1ed1ng n\u01b0\u1edbc; ng\u01b0\u1eddi h\u1ecdc mang towel."),i(8,"Buy lunch at the ______.",["cafe","caf\xe9"],"Cafe c\u1ea1nh l\u1ed1i v\xe0o ph\u1ee5c v\u1ee5 b\u1eefa tr\u01b0a."),i(9,"Boats return at: ______",["12:15","12.15","twelve fifteen"],"Thuy\u1ec1n v\u1ec1 l\xfac 12:15.",2),i(10,"Payment method: ______ only",["card"],"Ch\u1ec9 thanh to\xe1n b\u1eb1ng card, kh\xf4ng nh\u1eadn ti\u1ec1n m\u1eb7t.")]},{id:"community-garden",title:"The garden around the corner",subtitle:"M\u1ed9t bu\u1ed5i gi\u1edbi thi\u1ec7u v\u01b0\u1eddn c\u1ed9ng \u0111\u1ed3ng",part:2,format:"Note completion + matching",instructions:"Questions 1\u20136: write ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER. Questions 7\u201310: match each activity to its location.",media:s("community-garden"),transcript:"Welcome to Willow Community Garden. I am pleased to see so many new volunteers here today. Before we walk around, I will explain how the project works.\nThe garden opened in twenty eighteen on land that had previously been used as a car park. It is now run by volunteers rather than the town council. Anyone can visit during the day, but volunteering sessions usually take place on Wednesday mornings. Our old Saturday group no longer meets.\nYou do not need to buy equipment. Tools are kept in the shed beside the entrance. Please return them clean at the end of each session. You should, however, bring your own gloves. We provide drinking water, but we do not keep spare gloves in different sizes.\nOur youngest volunteers work in small groups with an adult. The minimum age for these sessions is twelve. Anyone younger can still visit with their family, but cannot join a working group.\nIf rain becomes heavy while we are working, we will move into the greenhouse. Please do not shelter under the large trees during a storm. The coordinator will tell you when outdoor work can begin again.\nNow for four activities on our next open day. At the entrance, visitors can exchange seeds. There will be labelled boxes so that everyone knows what they are taking home.\nThe pond is the best place for the wildlife activity. A volunteer will help children identify insects there, using picture cards rather than collecting live animals.\nBeside the apple trees, the tasting activity will offer fruit grown in the garden. This is a chance to compare varieties, not a competition.\nFinally, tool repair will take place at the workshop. An experienced volunteer will demonstrate safe ways to maintain equipment. Please book a place because space inside is limited.",questions:[i(1,"The garden opened in ______.",["2018"],"Nghe twenty eighteen, t\u1ee9c 2018."),i(2,"Regular volunteering day: ______",["Wednesday"],"Nh\xf3m Saturday c\u0169 \u0111\xe3 ng\u1eebng; l\u1ecbch hi\u1ec7n t\u1ea1i l\xe0 Wednesday."),i(3,"Tools are stored in the ______.",["shed"],"D\u1ee5ng c\u1ee5 n\u1eb1m trong shed c\u1ea1nh l\u1ed1i v\xe0o."),i(4,"Volunteers must bring their own ______.",["gloves"],"T\u1ef1 mang g\u0103ng tay, kh\xf4ng ph\u1ea3i d\u1ee5ng c\u1ee5."),i(5,"Minimum age to join a working group: ______",["12","twelve"],"Tu\u1ed5i t\u1ed1i thi\u1ec3u l\xe0 12; tr\u1ebb h\u01a1n ch\u1ec9 tham quan c\xf9ng gia \u0111\xecnh."),i(6,"In heavy rain, move into the ______.",["greenhouse"],"Khi m\u01b0a to chuy\u1ec3n v\xe0o greenhouse."),...[["Seed exchange","A \xb7 entrance"],["Wildlife identification","C \xb7 pond"],["Fruit tasting","D \xb7 apple trees"],["Tool repair","B \xb7 workshop"]].map(([t,n],i)=>o(i+7,`Where will ${t.toLowerCase()} take place?`,["A \xb7 entrance","B \xb7 workshop","C \xb7 pond","D \xb7 apple trees","E \xb7 greenhouse"],n,`B\xe0i n\xf3i \u0111\u1eb7t ${t} \u1edf ${n.split(" \xb7 ")[1]}.`,"matching"))]},{id:"student-research",title:"Designing a better survey",subtitle:"Hai sinh vi\xean trao \u0111\u1ed5i v\u1ec1 d\u1ef1 \xe1n nghi\xean c\u1ee9u",part:3,format:"Multiple choice + sentence completion",instructions:"Questions 1\u20135: choose ONE answer. Questions 6\u201310: write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER.",media:s("student-research"),transcript:"Maya: We need to agree on our research project. We originally considered recycling in student flats, but we have now chosen to investigate how students use the library.\nLeo: Right. I liked the recycling idea, but another group had already chosen it. The library topic gives us something different to explore.\nMaya: Our tutor suggested we focus on evening visits. She said studying every service would make the project too broad. We can still ask why students come, but only after six o'clock.\nLeo: How should we recruit participants? Emailing the whole university would take too long to organise.\nMaya: Let us approach students at the library exit. That way, we will speak to people who have actually used it that evening.\nLeo: Good idea. The first draft of the questionnaire asks whether people like the excellent new lighting. That sounds biased.\nMaya: I agree. We should use neutral language and ask how they would rate the lighting, without describing it as excellent.\nLeo: We also need to test the questions before the main study.\nMaya: Yes. A small pilot should show us whether any question is confusing. We are not using the pilot to estimate the final results.\nLeo: How many people should take part in the pilot?\nMaya: Eight students should be enough. After that, we will revise the questionnaire and collect the main responses over three evenings.\nLeo: Shall we make audio recordings of their answers?\nMaya: No. The tutor asked us to use paper forms. Students can complete them quickly, and we will enter the results later.\nLeo: Where do we keep those forms?\nMaya: In the locked cabinet in our department. We must not leave them on a desk. And we should not collect names, because we want the responses to remain anonymous.\nLeo: Finally, when is the report due?\nMaya: On Friday. We should finish analysing the data on Thursday so we have time to check the writing.",questions:[o(1,"What topic have the students selected?",["Library use","Recycling in flats","Student transport"],"Library use","Recycling l\xe0 \xfd t\u01b0\u1edfng c\u0169; l\u1ef1a ch\u1ecdn cu\u1ed1i l\xe0 c\xe1ch d\xf9ng th\u01b0 vi\u1ec7n."),o(2,"What does the tutor recommend?",["Studying every library service","Focusing on evening visits","Comparing several universities"],"Focusing on evening visits","Gi\u1edbi h\u1ea1n ph\u1ea1m vi \u1edf l\u01b0\u1ee3t \u0111\u1ebfn bu\u1ed5i t\u1ed1i."),o(3,"Where will they recruit participants?",["Through a university-wide email","At the library exit","In their own classroom"],"At the library exit","H\u1ecd s\u1ebd ti\u1ebfp c\u1eadn sinh vi\xean t\u1ea1i l\u1ed1i ra th\u01b0 vi\u1ec7n."),o(4,"What is wrong with the lighting question?",["It contains biased wording","It has no answer space","It asks about the wrong building"],"It contains biased wording","T\u1eeb excellent d\u1eabn d\u1eaft ng\u01b0\u1eddi tr\u1ea3 l\u1eddi; c\u1ea7n di\u1ec5n \u0111\u1ea1t trung l\u1eadp."),o(5,"What is the purpose of the pilot?",["To produce the final results","To find confusing questions","To advertise the library"],"To find confusing questions","Pilot \u0111\u1ec3 ph\xe1t hi\u1ec7n c\xe2u h\u1ecfi g\xe2y kh\xf3 hi\u1ec3u."),i(6,"Pilot group size: ______ students",["8","eight"],"Nh\xf3m pilot g\u1ed3m 8 sinh vi\xean.",2),i(7,"Responses will be collected using ______.",["paper forms"],"D\xf9ng paper forms, kh\xf4ng ghi \xe2m.",2),i(8,"Store the responses in a locked ______.",["cabinet"],"T\u1ee7 c\xf3 kh\xf3a l\xe0 cabinet.",2),i(9,"Do not collect participants\u2019 ______.",["names"],"Kh\xf4ng thu t\xean \u0111\u1ec3 gi\u1eef c\xe2u tr\u1ea3 l\u1eddi \u1ea9n danh.",2),i(10,"Report deadline: ______",["Friday"],"Friday l\xe0 h\u1ea1n n\u1ed9p; Thursday ch\u1ec9 l\xe0 ng\xe0y ho\xe0n t\u1ea5t ph\xe2n t\xedch.",2)]},{id:"urban-trees",title:"What urban trees really do",subtitle:"B\xe0i gi\u1ea3ng ng\u1eafn v\u1ec1 c\xe2y xanh \u0111\xf4 th\u1ecb",part:4,format:"Summary completion",instructions:"Complete the lecture summary. Write ONE WORD ONLY in each gap.",media:s("urban-trees"),transcript:"Today we will look at the role of trees in the urban environment. The first benefit is familiar to anyone who has walked through a park in summer. Tree canopies provide shade, reducing the amount of direct sunlight that reaches people and surfaces below them.\nTrees also release water through their leaves. Evaporation of this water helps cool the surrounding air. This process is different from shade, although the two effects can work together.\nBelow ground, roots help water move into the soil. In a built-up area, many surfaces are covered with concrete or asphalt, so rain cannot enter the ground easily. Trees and other planting can therefore help manage rainfall. They do not, however, remove the need for well-designed drainage systems.\nOne important limit on a tree's growth is the space available for its roots. If that space is too small, even regular watering may not solve the problem. Designers need to think about the tree's mature size, not just its size on the day it is planted.\nChoosing a range of species can make a city's tree population less vulnerable to disease. If all the trees belong to one species, a single disease may affect a large proportion of them. Diversity spreads that risk.\nMaintenance is equally important. In their early years, young trees often need additional watering during dry weather. A planting programme should therefore include a budget for care after planting.\nFor researchers, evaluating the effects of urban trees requires careful measurement. Temperature sensors can be placed in both planted and unplanted areas. Measurements should be collected over several seasons rather than during only one hot afternoon.\nSocial access also matters. People with limited mobility may find a park difficult to use if its paths are narrow or uneven. Accessible design needs to be part of the plan from the beginning.\nTo conclude, counting newly planted trees is not enough to evaluate success. Researchers should also record survival. A smaller number of healthy, established trees can provide more lasting benefits than a large planting effort that is not maintained.",cloze:"Tree canopies provide {{q1}}. Water is released through the {{q2}}, helping cool the air. Below ground, {{q3}} help rain enter the soil. Growth can be limited by a lack of {{q4}}.\n\nUsing different tree species reduces the risk from {{q5}}. Young trees may require extra {{q6}} in dry periods. Researchers measure temperature with {{q7}} and collect data across several {{q8}}.\n\nA park\u2019s {{q9}} should be accessible to people with limited mobility. To evaluate a planting programme, measure tree {{q10}} rather than simply counting new trees.",questions:[i(1,"Canopies provide ______.",["shade"],"Ph\u1ea7n m\u1edf \u0111\u1ea7u n\xeau c\xe2y t\u1ea1o b\xf3ng r\xe2m: shade."),i(2,"Water is released through the ______.",["leaves"],"N\u01b0\u1edbc tho\xe1t qua leaves, kh\xf4ng ph\u1ea3i roots."),i(3,"______ help water enter the soil.",["roots"],"Roots gi\xfap n\u01b0\u1edbc th\u1ea5m v\xe0o \u0111\u1ea5t."),i(4,"Root growth requires sufficient ______.",["space"],"Y\u1ebfu t\u1ed1 gi\u1edbi h\u1ea1n \u0111\u01b0\u1ee3c nh\u1ea5n m\u1ea1nh l\xe0 space."),i(5,"Diversity reduces the risk from ______.",["disease"],"Nhi\u1ec1u lo\xe0i c\xe2y gi\xfap ph\xe2n t\xe1n r\u1ee7i ro b\u1ec7nh."),i(6,"Young trees need extra ______ in dry weather.",["watering"],"C\u1ea7n watering trong th\u1eddi k\u1ef3 kh\xf4 h\u1ea1n."),i(7,"Temperature is measured with ______.",["sensors"],"D\xf9ng temperature sensors."),i(8,"Data should cover several ______.",["seasons"],"\u0110o qua nhi\u1ec1u seasons thay v\xec m\u1ed9t bu\u1ed5i chi\u1ec1u n\xf3ng."),i(9,"Accessible ______ help people with limited mobility.",["paths"],"Paths h\u1eb9p ho\u1eb7c g\u1ed3 gh\u1ec1 l\xe0m kh\xf3 ng\u01b0\u1eddi h\u1ea1n ch\u1ebf v\u1eadn \u0111\u1ed9ng."),i(10,"A useful measure of success is tree ______.",["survival"],"\u0110\xe1nh gi\xe1 survival thay v\xec ch\u1ec9 \u0111\u1ebfm s\u1ed1 c\xe2y tr\u1ed3ng m\u1edbi.")]}],...t.expandedListening,...n.clozeListening]},244,[245,246]);
__d(function(g,r,_i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"expandedListening",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return o}});var t=r(d[0]);const o=[["pottery-workshop","A place at the pottery table","\u0110\u0103ng k\xfd l\u1edbp l\xe0m g\u1ed1m cu\u1ed1i tu\u1ea7n",1,"Good afternoon, Clay Corner Studio. Are you calling about a course? Yes, I would like to join the introductory pottery workshop. My surname is Morgan, spelled M O R G A N. I saw a class advertised for Monday, but I cannot come then. That is our regular class. The beginner workshop is on Sunday, so perhaps that would suit you better. Yes, Sunday is perfect. What time does it start? At ten, although the studio opens half an hour earlier. You can come early to look around, but teaching begins at ten.\nHow much does it cost? Thirty six pounds per person. That includes the clay and the use of tools, but you should bring an apron. We have a few spare ones, though they may not fit everybody. Where is the studio? On Park Road, opposite the bank. Please use the side entrance because the front door is being repaired. Your teacher is Nina. She will show you how to make a small bowl before you try anything larger.\nCan I take the finished bowl home that day? No, it needs to dry and go into the kiln. Collection will be on Thursday. We will send a message if anything changes. Finally, do I need to pay now? Yes, please pay by transfer to confirm your place. We cannot reserve it until payment arrives. That is fine. I will do it this afternoon.","Surname: [[Morgan]]. Beginner workshop day: [[Sunday]]. Teaching starts at [[ten]]. Price: \xa3[[36]]. Bring an [[apron]]. Location: [[Park]] Road. Use the [[side]] entrance. Teacher: [[Nina]]. Collection day: [[Thursday]]. Pay by [[transfer]]."],["bike-repair","Getting a bicycle repaired","G\u1ecdi c\u1eeda h\xe0ng s\u1eeda xe \u0111\u1ea1p",1,"Hello, Green Wheel Repairs. How can I help you? My bicycle is making a strange noise and I would like someone to look at it. Of course. Could I have your surname? Bennett, with two letters T at the end. And what colour is the bicycle? It is silver. I have a black one too, but that belongs to my sister. What seems to be wrong? The noise happens when I use the brakes. I thought it was the chain at first, but the chain seems fine.\nCan you bring it in on Tuesday? I am working that day. Would Wednesday be possible? Yes, we have an appointment at eleven on Wednesday. We do a basic inspection for eighteen pounds. If new parts are needed, we will tell you the cost before doing the work. Please do not replace anything without calling me. Certainly. Would you like us to contact you by phone or email? Email is best because I cannot answer calls while I am teaching.\nYou can leave the bike at our workshop on Mill Street. The main entrance is next to the florist. Do not use the delivery gate at the back. Please bring the key for the wheel lock so we can test everything. If the repair is simple, you can collect it on Friday. We close at six that day. There is no collection service over the weekend, so let us know if Friday is difficult.","Surname: [[Bennett]]. Bicycle colour: [[silver]]. Problem with the [[brakes]]. Appointment day: [[Wednesday]]. Appointment time: [[eleven]]. Inspection costs \xa3[[18]]. Contact by [[email]]. Workshop on [[Mill]] Street. Bring the [[key]]. Expected collection: [[Friday]]."],["homestay-booking","Finding a room for the course","Trao \u0111\u1ed5i v\u1ec1 ch\u1ed7 \u1edf khi du h\u1ecdc",1,"Welcome to the student accommodation office. My name is Adam. How can I help? I am looking for a homestay while I attend the summer language course. My family name is Patel. Thank you. How long will you be staying? Six weeks altogether. The course is only five weeks, but I want an extra week to explore the area. Would you prefer a single room or a shared room? A single room, please. I need somewhere quiet to study in the evenings.\nWe have a family in the north of the town, close to the college. Their home is about twenty minutes away by bus. The weekly charge is one hundred and forty pounds, including breakfast. Dinner costs extra, and lunch is not provided. Breakfast is enough for me because I usually eat at college during the day. Do you have any dietary requirements? Yes, I am vegetarian. Please make a note of that.\nThe room has a desk and a wardrobe. You should bring your own towels, although bedding is provided. There is wireless internet throughout the house. The family has a dog, but no cats. Is that a problem? No, I like dogs. Good. To reserve the room, we need a deposit of fifty pounds. We will then email the full address and arrival instructions. Do not send the whole six weeks' payment yet; the balance is due after you arrive.","Family name: [[Patel]]. Stay for [[six]] weeks. Preferred room: [[single]]. House in the [[north]] of town. Travel to college by [[bus]]. Weekly cost: \xa3[[140]]. Meal included: [[breakfast]]. Diet: [[vegetarian]]. Bring your own [[towels]]. Deposit: \xa3[[50]]."],["museum-tour","An evening at the museum","H\u01b0\u1edbng d\u1eabn kh\xe1ch tham quan b\u1ea3o t\xe0ng",2,"Good evening and welcome to the Harbour Museum. Before we begin the tour, I would like to explain a few practical details. The museum normally closes at five, but tonight it will stay open until eight. Your ticket allows you to return to any gallery after our guided tour finishes. Please keep it with you throughout your visit.\nLarge bags must be left in the lockers downstairs. Small handbags are allowed in the galleries. The temporary exhibition is on the second floor. You can take the stairs near the ticket desk, or use the lift if you prefer. The exhibition focuses on fishing communities rather than famous ships. Some of the photographs have been lent by local families and have never been displayed publicly before.\nIf you would like an audio guide, collect one from reception. It is included in the ticket price. The children's activity begins in the courtyard at six. Children will make simple models from paper, so there is no need to bring materials. An adult must stay with each child.\nThe cafe is beside the garden and will serve light meals until seven. Drinking water is available there throughout the evening. Photography is permitted, but flash is not allowed because it can damage sensitive objects. The shop sells postcards and books. Any profits from tonight's postcard sales will support our restoration work. Finally, if you become separated from your group, please meet at the clock near the entrance.","Tonight's closing time: [[eight]]. Large bags go in the [[lockers]]. Exhibition on the [[second]] floor. Main subject: [[fishing]] communities. Audio guides from [[reception]]. Children's activity in the [[courtyard]]. Models made from [[paper]]. Cafe beside the [[garden]]. Do not use [[flash]]. Meeting point: the [[clock]]."],["walking-trail","A new trail through the reserve","Gi\u1edbi thi\u1ec7u tuy\u1ebfn \u0111i b\u1ed9 trong khu b\u1ea3o t\u1ed3n",2,"Thank you for joining today's introductory walk. The new trail is four kilometres long and follows a circular route, so we will finish where we started. It usually takes about ninety minutes, including short stops. The path is suitable for beginners, but the ground can become slippery after rain. Please wear boots rather than open sandals.\nOur first stop will be at the pond. We will look for signs of animal activity without disturbing the water. Further along, we will cross a wooden bridge. Only three people should stand on the bridge at one time, so please wait for my signal before crossing. The old metal bridge is closed and should not be used.\nThe best viewpoint is at the tower. You can climb the steps if you wish, but this part of the visit is optional. There are benches at the bottom for anyone who prefers to rest. Please bring your own water because there are no taps along the route. Snacks are fine, but all litter must be carried out of the reserve.\nDogs are allowed on the main trail if they stay on leads. They cannot enter the small wildlife enclosure. If you hear a whistle, stop and listen to your guide. Three short whistles mean that the group needs to gather immediately. Our volunteers meet every month to maintain the path. The next maintenance day is Saturday, and anyone interested can register at the information desk after the walk.","Trail length: [[four]] kilometres. Allow [[ninety]] minutes. Wear [[boots]]. First stop: the [[pond]]. Cross the wooden [[bridge]]. Viewpoint at the [[tower]]. Bring your own [[water]]. Dogs must stay on [[leads]]. Stop when you hear a [[whistle]]. Next maintenance day: [[Saturday]]."],["arts-festival","Your guide to the arts weekend","Th\xf4ng b\xe1o ho\u1ea1t \u0111\u1ed9ng t\u1ea1i l\u1ec5 h\u1ed9i ngh\u1ec7 thu\u1eadt",2,"Welcome to our neighbourhood arts weekend. This is the seventh year of the event, and we are delighted that so many local artists are taking part. Entry to the outdoor displays is free. Some indoor workshops require a ticket because the rooms have limited space. You can buy tickets at the library, not at the individual venues.\nThe opening performance will take place in the square at noon. It features a small orchestra made up of local students. If it rains heavily, the performance will move to the school hall. We will announce any change on the noticeboard beside the main entrance.\nThere are several activities for families. The painting workshop is in the tent near the fountain. Children should wear old clothes because the paint may not wash out easily. The photography walk starts at the station and lasts about an hour. Participants only need a phone; expensive cameras are not required. The pottery demonstration is at the community centre, where visitors can watch an artist working at the wheel.\nFood stalls are located behind the theatre. Please use the recycling bins provided, and return reusable cups to the collection point. Bicycle parking is available by the church. Cars must use the public car park outside the festival area. Volunteers wearing yellow badges can help you find your way around. Finally, the closing concert begins at seven on Sunday. We hope you enjoy discovering the creative work being made in your own neighbourhood.","This is the [[seventh]] festival. Buy workshop tickets at the [[library]]. Opening performance in the [[square]]. Rain venue: school [[hall]]. Painting workshop in a [[tent]]. Photography walk starts at the [[station]]. Pottery at the community [[centre]]. Food behind the [[theatre]]. Volunteers wear [[yellow]] badges. Closing concert day: [[Sunday]]."],["sleep-project","Planning a student sleep diary","Thi\u1ebft k\u1ebf nh\u1eadt k\xfd gi\u1ea5c ng\u1ee7 cho nghi\xean c\u1ee9u",3,"We need to finalise our project before meeting the tutor. Originally we wanted to compare exercise routines, but we have decided to investigate sleep. Yes, and we should keep the sample small. The tutor suggested twenty participants rather than the fifty we first proposed. That should make the data easier to manage within our deadline.\nHow will we collect information? I think a diary would work better than one long questionnaire. Participants can record what happened each morning, while they still remember the night clearly. We should ask them to complete the diary for fourteen days. A single weekend would not show the difference between busy and quiet days.\nWhat should they record? Bedtime, waking time and whether they drank coffee in the evening. We should also include a simple mood rating. We must not ask for their medical records. That would go beyond the purpose of our student project. To protect privacy, everyone will use a number instead of their name.\nWe can store the completed diaries in a password-protected folder. Only the two of us and our tutor should have access. For the presentation, a bar chart could compare average sleep duration on weekdays and weekends. We should explain that our small sample cannot represent all students. Exactly. Let's prepare the information sheet first. The tutor wants to see that on Monday. After approval, we can begin recruiting volunteers and explain that they may withdraw at any time.","Research topic: [[sleep]]. Number of participants: [[twenty]]. Data tool: a [[diary]]. Complete it each [[morning]]. Study duration: [[fourteen]] days. Record evening [[coffee]] consumption. Include a [[mood]] rating. Identify participants by [[number]]. Present averages in a [[bar]] chart. Information sheet due: [[Monday]]."],["canteen-project","Reducing waste in the canteen","Th\u1ea3o lu\u1eadn c\xe1ch kh\u1ea3o s\xe1t r\xe1c th\u1ef1c ph\u1ea9m",3,"Let's review the plan for our canteen waste project. We need to distinguish food left on plates from waste produced in the kitchen. Our project will focus on plates because the kitchen already keeps its own records. That makes sense. Should we observe breakfast and lunch? The tutor advised us to concentrate on lunch, when the canteen is busiest.\nWe will collect data for five days, starting next week. A member of staff will separate the waste, so we do not handle it directly. We can use scales to measure the total weight at the end of each lunch period. Counting bins would not be accurate because they are rarely filled to the same level.\nWe should record the number of meals served as well. Then we can calculate waste per meal instead of comparing daily totals alone. Let's also note the menu, because some dishes may produce more waste than others. We should not conclude that a dish is unpopular just because it has heavier leftovers; it might contain bones or peel.\nFor our interviews, we will speak to the manager and two serving staff. We do not need to question every student. Our suggested intervention is to offer smaller portions, with the option of asking for more. The manager wants recommendations by Wednesday. We can prepare a poster to explain the results, but we should avoid photographs of identifiable customers. The aim is to improve the service, not to blame individual diners.","Focus on waste from [[plates]]. Observe the [[lunch]] service. Collect data for [[five]] days. Measure weight with [[scales]]. Record meals and the daily [[menu]]. Interview the [[manager]] and serving staff. Suggest smaller [[portions]]. Recommendations due: [[Wednesday]]. Present results on a [[poster]]. Avoid identifiable customer [[photographs]]."],["transport-project","Comparing journeys to campus","Nghi\xean c\u1ee9u h\xe0nh tr\xecnh \u0111\u1ebfn tr\u01b0\u1eddng",3,"Our transport project needs a clearer research question. Instead of asking about every form of travel, let's compare bus journeys with cycling. We can recruit volunteers from our department, but we should explain that this will not be a representative sample of the whole university. The tutor suggested twelve volunteers, with six in each group.\nEach person should record the time when they leave home and the time when they reach campus. We must include waiting time for the bus, not just the minutes spent on board. Otherwise the comparison would be misleading. Cyclists should include time spent parking and locking their bicycles.\nWe will repeat the measurements on three separate days. Let's choose days with normal teaching schedules rather than examination days. The weather should be recorded because heavy rain may affect both routes and travel choices. We also need the distance of each journey, so we can avoid treating a very short trip as directly comparable to a long one.\nHow will participants submit their information? An online form would be convenient. They can use a code instead of their names. We should not collect home addresses; approximate distance is enough. For the final presentation, a table can show the median journey time for each group. We should discuss the limits of the comparison before making recommendations. Our slides are due on Thursday. Let's send the draft questions to the tutor tomorrow so there is time to revise them.","Compare buses with [[cycling]]. Recruit [[twelve]] volunteers. Include bus [[waiting]] time. Cyclists include time spent [[parking]]. Measure on [[three]] days. Record the [[weather]] and journey [[distance]]. Submit an online [[form]]. Show results in a [[table]]. Slides due: [[Thursday]]."],["seed-study","Keeping a seed collection useful","B\xe0i gi\u1ea3ng t\xecnh hu\u1ed1ng: qu\u1ea3n l\xfd b\u1ed9 s\u01b0u t\u1eadp h\u1ea1t",4,"Today I will describe a fictional seed collection project used in our training course. The purpose of the exercise is to explore how careful records support conservation work. In the project, volunteers collect seeds from several garden plots. Each sample receives a label before it leaves the collection site. Without that label, later measurements would be difficult to connect to the correct plant.\nThe team records the date and the location of collection. It also notes whether the parent plant appeared healthy. Seeds are placed in paper bags during transport. Plastic bags are avoided in this exercise because moisture could remain trapped inside. At the laboratory, the samples are dried before they are placed in storage.\nThe training protocol uses sealed containers kept in a cool room. A sensor records the temperature, while staff check the equipment regularly. The collection is not simply left untouched. Every year, a small sample is tested for germination. Staff count how many seeds begin to grow under the test conditions. A fall in germination may indicate that a stored sample needs attention.\nStudents compare records from two collection seasons. They are asked to identify gaps in the documentation before interpreting any differences. The final report includes a map showing where the samples came from. The exercise does not claim that one storage method is suitable for every species. Its main lesson is that a collection becomes more useful when the material and the records can be trusted together.","Give each sample a [[label]]. Record collection date and [[location]]. Transport seeds in [[paper]] bags. Samples are [[dried]] before storage. Use sealed [[containers]]. Monitor the [[temperature]]. Test germination every [[year]]. Count seeds that start to [[grow]]. Compare two collection [[seasons]]. Include a [[map]] in the report."],["lighting-study","Testing a quieter street at night","B\xe0i gi\u1ea3ng t\xecnh hu\u1ed1ng: th\u1eed nghi\u1ec7m \u0111\xe8n \u0111\u01b0\u1eddng",4,"This lecture presents an invented case study about a town reviewing its street lighting. The council wants to understand whether a change in lighting can reduce energy use without making pedestrians feel less comfortable. The study compares two streets with similar traffic levels. One street keeps its existing lamps, while the other receives new controls.\nBefore making changes, the team collects baseline measurements for a month. Electricity use is recorded at the meter. Researchers also count pedestrians at fixed times, because an unusually quiet evening could influence people's opinions. The new controls reduce brightness after midnight, but the lamps do not switch off completely.\nResidents are invited to complete a survey. The questions ask about visibility, comfort and whether people avoid particular parts of the street. Researchers avoid describing the new system as either safer or more dangerous. That wording could influence the responses. Participants do not provide their names, and the published report contains only grouped results.\nThe team also photographs the street from fixed positions. A camera is placed at the same height each time, and the exposure settings are kept constant. Rainy and dry evenings are recorded separately because reflections can change the appearance of the pavement. After three months, the researchers compare the results with the baseline. They recommend further testing before the council changes lighting across the entire town. The case illustrates why both measurements and residents' experiences matter in evaluating a public service.","Compare [[two]] streets. Baseline period: one [[month]]. Electricity measured at the [[meter]]. Count [[pedestrians]] at fixed times. Reduce brightness after [[midnight]]. Residents complete a [[survey]]. Do not collect [[names]]. Keep camera [[height]] constant. Record rainy and [[dry]] evenings separately. Review after [[three]] months."],["archive-study","Making a community archive searchable","B\xe0i gi\u1ea3ng t\xecnh hu\u1ed1ng: s\u1ed1 h\xf3a t\u01b0 li\u1ec7u \u0111\u1ecba ph\u01b0\u01a1ng",4,"Our final case study concerns a fictional community archive. The archive holds photographs, letters and recordings donated by residents. Its first digital project focuses on photographs because volunteers already have experience scanning them. The team begins by assigning each item a unique identifier. This number links the digital image to the physical object.\nBefore scanning, volunteers check the condition of each photograph. Damaged material is referred to a specialist rather than being flattened by hand. The original photographs remain in protective sleeves after the work is complete. Digital files are saved in two versions: a high-quality master and a smaller copy for the website.\nDescriptions include a date where one is known, and a location if it can be identified reliably. Volunteers are instructed not to guess missing names. Uncertain details are marked as unknown until more evidence is available. A second volunteer reviews each description before publication. This review is intended to catch errors, not to remove different interpretations of an image.\nThe archive obtains permission from donors before publishing material online. Some items remain accessible only in the reading room. Backups are stored in a separate building so that one local incident is less likely to affect every copy. The project team measures success partly through search tasks completed by visitors. Download numbers alone would not show whether people can find the material they need. The case reminds us that digitisation involves organisation and judgement, not simply operating a scanner.","First digitise [[photographs]]. Assign a unique [[identifier]]. Check each item's [[condition]]. Keep originals in protective [[sleeves]]. Save a master and a website [[copy]]. Do not guess missing [[names]]. A second volunteer performs a [[review]]. Obtain donor [[permission]]. Keep backups in another [[building]]. Evaluate visitors' [[search]] tasks."]].map(([o,n,i,s,h,l])=>{const c=(0,t.parseCloze)(l);return{id:o,title:n,subtitle:i,part:s,format:1===s?"Form completion":4===s?"Summary completion":"Note completion",instructions:"Complete the notes. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER in each gap.",media:{kind:"bundled",src:`/task1-coach-pages/listening/${o}.mp3`},transcript:h,cloze:c.cloze,questions:c.questions.map((t,o)=>({...t,explanation:`C\xe2u ${o+1}: th\xf4ng tin c\u1ea7n nghe l\xe0 \u201c${t.answers[0]}\u201d. \u0110\u1ed1i chi\u1ebfu chi ti\u1ebft t\u01b0\u01a1ng \u1ee9ng trong transcript; kh\xf4ng ch\u1ecdn th\xf4ng tin b\u1ecb s\u1eeda ho\u1eb7c lo\u1ea1i tr\u1eeb.`}))}})},245,[229]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"clozeListening",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return n}});var t=r(d[0]);const n=[["swimming-club","Joining the swimming club","\u0110\u0103ng k\xfd c\xe2u l\u1ea1c b\u1ed9 b\u01a1i",1,"Good morning. I am calling to ask about the beginners' swimming class. My surname is [[Wilson]]. I can come on weekdays, but not at the weekend. The class you need meets on [[Tuesday]] and starts at [[seven]] in the evening. It is held in the [[small]] pool, away from the children's session. The course lasts for [[eight]] weeks and costs [[64]] pounds altogether. Please bring a [[towel]] and your swimming clothes. Your instructor is [[David]], who will meet you at reception before the first lesson. If you are travelling by car, use the parking area behind the [[library]]. Please arrive early so there is time to complete the registration form. To confirm the booking, we need your payment by [[Friday]]. We will send you a receipt and full directions after payment arrives."],["train-information","A change to the rail journey","Th\xf4ng b\xe1o thay \u0111\u1ed5i chuy\u1ebfn t\xe0u",2,"May I have your attention, please? The service to [[Brighton]] will leave from platform [[four]] today, not its usual platform. Departure is delayed by [[twenty]] minutes because of a signalling problem. Passengers should remain behind the [[yellow]] line while waiting. The front two coaches are reserved for [[first]] class passengers. A member of staff will help anyone who needs assistance boarding. Please meet that member of staff near the [[lift]]. Large luggage can be placed on the racks at the end of each coach, but bicycles must go in the [[rear]] coach. The buffet will sell drinks and [[sandwiches]], although hot meals are unavailable today. Tickets will be checked after the first stop. If you require a refund form, collect one from the [[office]] beside the entrance. We apologise for the inconvenience and expect normal services to resume on [[Monday]]."],["camping-trip","Preparing for the camping weekend","Chu\u1ea9n b\u1ecb chuy\u1ebfn c\u1eafm tr\u1ea1i",2,"Here are the final arrangements for our camping weekend. We will leave on [[Saturday]], meeting outside the school [[gate]]. The coach departs at [[eight]], so please be there fifteen minutes earlier. Our campsite is beside a [[lake]], but swimming will not be part of this trip. Each student should bring a sleeping bag and a [[torch]]. Tents will be provided, and you will share in groups of [[three]]. The first evening meal is [[pasta]], with a vegetarian option available. Please tell the organiser about any dietary requirements before we leave. During the day we will follow a marked [[trail]] through the woods. Wear shoes suitable for uneven ground and carry your own water. In case of heavy rain, group activities will move into the campsite [[shelter]]. We expect to return home on Sunday at [[five]], although traffic may affect the exact arrival time."],["language-exchange","Meeting a language partner","Tham gia bu\u1ed5i trao \u0111\u1ed5i ng\xf4n ng\u1eef",1,"Hello, I would like to join the language exchange. My first name is [[Sofia]], and I am currently studying [[French]]. I can help another learner with English in return. The weekly meeting takes place at the [[cafe]] inside the community centre. It begins at [[six]] on Thursday evenings. New members should look for a volunteer wearing a [[green]] badge. There is no membership fee, but each person buys their own drink. We usually work in [[pairs]], changing partners halfway through the evening. Please bring a [[notebook]] if you want to record useful expressions. This month's discussion theme is [[travel]], and you may bring a photograph to help start a conversation. The meeting lasts for [[ninety]] minutes. To reserve your place, send an email before [[Wednesday]]. You will receive a short confirmation message; please check your spam folder if it does not arrive."],["volunteer-fair","A volunteer fair on campus","Ng\xe0y h\u1ed9i t\xecnh nguy\u1ec7n",2,"Welcome to the campus volunteer fair. There are [[twelve]] organisations taking part this year. Their information tables are in the main [[hall]], and entry is free. The first short talk begins at [[ten]] and explains how students can fit volunteering around their studies. If you are interested in working outdoors, visit the [[garden]] project table near the windows. The reading support programme asks for a commitment of [[two]] hours each week. All new reading volunteers receive [[training]] before meeting participants. You do not need to bring a printed application today; you can register using a [[tablet]] at each table. Please ask a volunteer if you need help entering your details. A general question session will take place after [[lunch]]. Free water is available beside the [[stairs]]. The fair closes at [[four]], but the website will keep the organisations' contact details available afterwards."],["group-presentation","Organising a group presentation","Ph\xe2n c\xf4ng b\xe0i thuy\u1ebft tr\xecnh nh\xf3m",3,"We have to prepare our group presentation about [[tourism]]. Our tutor has asked us to focus on one town rather than a whole country. Let's use [[maps]] to explain where the main attractions are. The presentation should last [[ten]] minutes, followed by questions. Anna will research visitor [[numbers]], and Ben will look at employment. I will prepare the opening and the [[conclusion]]. We should use no more than [[six]] slides so that we do not rush through them. The tutor said that every figure needs a clear [[source]]. We cannot simply copy numbers without explaining where they came from. Let's rehearse in the [[library]] on Tuesday afternoon. During the rehearsal, one person can use a [[timer]] while the others speak. We will send the final slides by [[Wednesday]], leaving one day to deal with any technical problems before the class presentation."],["interview-project","Designing an interview guide","Thi\u1ebft k\u1ebf c\xe2u h\u1ecfi ph\u1ecfng v\u1ea5n",3,"For our project, we are interviewing people about their use of local [[parks]]. We need an interview guide with [[eight]] main questions. The questions should be [[open]], allowing people to explain their experiences instead of giving only yes or no answers. We will test the guide with [[three]] classmates before recruiting participants. Each interview should take around [[fifteen]] minutes. We must ask permission before making a [[recording]]. If someone does not agree, we can take notes instead. Participants will be identified by a [[code]], not by their real names. The files will be kept in a protected [[folder]] that only the project team can access. After the interviews, we will compare common [[themes]] in the responses. Our tutor wants the first analysis by [[Friday]]. We should mention differences between participants rather than trying to make every answer fit one simple conclusion."],["field-trip-budget","Keeping the field trip affordable","L\u1eadp ng\xe2n s\xe1ch chuy\u1ebfn th\u1ef1c \u0111\u1ecba",3,"We need to revise the budget for the class field trip to the [[coast]]. Hiring a coach is too expensive, so we will travel by [[train]]. The group ticket costs [[18]] pounds per student. We should bring a packed [[lunch]] rather than eat at a restaurant. The visitor centre offers a free room where we can review our notes if the weather is poor. Our main activity is to measure changes along the [[beach]]. We will work in groups of [[four]], with one person recording the results. Everyone should bring a [[pencil]], because ink may run if the notebook gets wet. We can borrow measuring equipment from the [[department]]. The trip is planned for [[Thursday]], but we will confirm after checking the weather. Students need to return their permission forms by [[Monday]]. Any student worried about the cost should speak privately to the course coordinator."],["local-newspaper","A digital edition for a local paper","B\xe0i n\xf3i v\u1ec1 b\xe1o \u0111\u1ecba ph\u01b0\u01a1ng",4,"This invented case study follows a local newspaper introducing a digital [[edition]]. The team begins by asking readers which sections they use most often. The survey shows strong interest in local [[events]], although the result represents only people who answered. The first website is designed around a simple [[menu]] rather than a crowded front page. Articles are checked by an [[editor]] before publication. Each story displays its publication [[date]], so readers can recognise older information. The paper also creates a clear process for submitting [[corrections]]. It does not silently replace a major error without noting the change. To understand reader experience, volunteers test the site on mobile [[phones]]. They record tasks that are difficult to complete and words that cause confusion. The team reviews these findings every [[month]]. Its initial goal is reliable [[access]], not the largest possible number of advertisements. Further development depends on reader feedback and the available [[budget]]."],["water-fountain","Testing a public water fountain","B\xe0i n\xf3i v\u1ec1 ti\u1ec7n \xedch c\xf4ng c\u1ed9ng",4,"In this fictional project, a council installs a drinking fountain beside a busy [[square]]. The team wants to understand how it is used before adding more fountains. A counter records the number of [[activations]], but this is not treated as the exact number of individual users. One person may press the button several times. Volunteers also observe the area during fixed [[periods]]. They note whether people fill a bottle or drink directly from the fountain. Maintenance staff check the equipment every [[morning]]. Problems are entered in a shared [[log]]. The project includes a small [[sign]] explaining how to report a fault. During the first review, residents ask for a lower button that is easier to [[reach]]. The council tests this change before ordering more equipment. The final report discusses both use and [[maintenance]]. It includes comments from nearby [[shops]] and recommends another review after [[winter]], when use may differ from the summer pattern."],["classroom-plants","Plants in a classroom study","C\xe2y xanh trong l\u1edbp h\u1ecdc",4,"Today's fictional classroom project asks students to compare two groups of [[plants]]. Both groups receive the same soil and the same amount of water. The variable being changed is the amount of [[light]]. Students measure growth using the same [[ruler]] and record the results every [[week]]. They agree on where each measurement should begin, because inconsistent methods could create misleading differences. Each pot has a unique [[number]], which links it to the record sheet. If a plant is damaged accidentally, the incident is noted rather than [[hidden]]. The class presents average growth in a [[graph]], but also shows the range of measurements. A large difference between individual plants may matter even when the averages look similar. The report includes a description of the [[method]] so another class could repeat it. The teacher asks students to distinguish an [[observation]] from an explanation. The final discussion proposes further tests instead of claiming that a single classroom experiment provides a universal [[answer]]."],["sports-equipment","Borrowing equipment at the sports centre","M\u01b0\u1ee3n thi\u1ebft b\u1ecb th\u1ec3 thao",1,"Hello, I would like to borrow equipment for the school sports afternoon. My surname is [[Harris]]. We need [[twelve]] badminton rackets and enough shuttlecocks for several games. The equipment is available on [[Friday]], but you must collect it before [[noon]]. Please bring a copy of the booking [[form]] and some identification. There is no hire charge for schools, although we require a deposit of [[40]] pounds. We return it when the equipment is checked in. Everything will be packed in a large [[bag]], so it should be easy to carry. Please do not leave the rackets outside in the rain. If anything is damaged, tell the person at [[reception]] when you return it. The deadline for returning the equipment is [[Monday]] morning. Our coordinator, [[Emma]], can answer any further questions. I will email the collection details now so you have the address and opening hours in writing."],["cookery-course","Choosing a cookery course","T\xecm l\u1edbp h\u1ecdc n\u1ea5u \u0103n",1,"I am interested in your evening cookery course. Could you tell me about the next one? Certainly. It starts in [[October]] and runs for [[six]] weeks. The class meets on [[Wednesday]] evenings. This course focuses on [[vegetarian]] meals that can be prepared at home without specialist equipment. The fee is [[90]] pounds, including ingredients. You should bring an [[apron]] and a container to take your food home. The teacher's name is [[Rose]]. She begins each lesson with a demonstration before students work at their own stations. The kitchen is on the [[ground]] floor, and there is an accessible entrance at the side of the building. We ask students to arrive [[ten]] minutes before the lesson starts. You can book online, but please contact the office first if you have any allergies. The final lesson includes a shared [[dinner]], giving everyone a chance to taste the dishes prepared by the group."],["exhibition-plan","Planning a student exhibition","L\xean k\u1ebf ho\u1ea1ch tri\u1ec3n l\xe3m sinh vi\xean",3,"Our exhibition will show student work about the local [[river]]. We have booked the community hall for [[Saturday]], and visitors will be admitted free. The display panels should be arranged around the [[walls]], leaving enough space in the centre for people to move. Each group needs a short [[title]] that visitors can read from a distance. We should print the explanations in a large [[font]], not squeeze too much text onto one page. The tutor suggested including a few [[photographs]] of our fieldwork. We must check that everyone shown has agreed to publication. A small table near the entrance will hold [[feedback]] cards. Visitors can leave comments anonymously. Ben will organise the [[lighting]], while Maya will check the labels. We should finish setting up by [[nine]] in the morning. After the event, the panels will be stored in the [[library]] so other classes can use them for discussion."]].map(([n,o,s,l,h])=>({id:n,title:o,subtitle:s,part:l,format:"Guided dictation",instructions:"Listen and fill each gap with ONE WORD OR A NUMBER. This guided transcript exercise gives more context than an exam paper.",media:{kind:"bundled",src:`/task1-coach-pages/listening/${n}.mp3`},...(0,t.parseCloze)(h)}))},246,[229]);
